   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Netherlands Enterprise Agency</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Hot forging of high-tensile industrial bolts and nuts from recycled steel</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Bolivian foundries and metalworking companies currently import their high-tensile industrial fasteners from hot forging companies in Brazil, Chile, Colombia and Peru. There exists a continuous demand for industrial fasteners in the end-user industries as the companies continuously need to replace the wearing parts of their production equipment, including the industrial fasteners. Additionally, each new production facility or network expansion in these sectors require new hardware components with its respective fasteners.The project aims to establish a company to produce hot forge high-tensile industrial bolts and nuts from steel bars made of recycled steel for the local and export market with a production capacity of 394 tons per year. The production facility will be certified according to the ISO 9001 and OHSAS 18001 standards. The joint venture will only purchase steel bars produced of scrap metal. Currently, scrap metal is wasted or collected and sold to Peru for US$180 per ton to be re-imported again as (semi) final products at a price of US$850 per ton (including US$200 transport cost). The project is located in Santa Cruz.The applicant Tec-Stam Forjaria e Estamparia Ltda was established in 1993 in Sao Paolo, Brazil, and the local partner Eduardo S.A. was established in 1973 in Santa Cruz.The project partners plan to establish a joint venture. No other company in Bolivia produces hot-forged high-tensile industrial fasteners of any kind from scrap metal.</narrative>
  </description>
